<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yuknoom Ch'een II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">reigned <a href="/years/636">636</a>–c. 686</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Yuknoom the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yuknoom Ch'een II, also called Yuknoom the Great, was the most powerful king of the Kaan, or snake-head, dynasty at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a>, ruling from 636 to about 686. He fought Tikal, controlled the rulers of cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dos-pilas">Dos Pilas</a>, Cancuen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naranjo">Naranjo</a>, and made Calakmul the leading power of the Classic Maya lowlands. Many of Calakmul's numerous stelae date from his reign and those of his successors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuknoom-cheen-ii.jpg" alt="Yuknoom Ch'een II" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard DUPONT · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Funerary_Mask_of_Yuknoom_Ch%27een_II_(Jade,_Omphacite,_Green_Quartz,_and_diverse_Shell)..._Calakmul,_Late_Classic_(600-800_AD).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yuknoom was born on 11 September 600. His parents were possibly King Scroll Serpent and his wife, Lady Scroll-in-hand. On his accession in 636 he took the name of the Early Classic king Yuknoom Ch'een I. Tikal was then showing strong signs of recovery from the defeat of its king Wak Chan K'awiil almost a hundred years earlier, and its royal line had split, with both B'alaj Chan K'awiil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dos-pilas">Dos Pilas</a> and his probable brother Nuun Ujol Chaak of Tikal styling themselves holy lords of Mutal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 650 the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dos-pilas">Dos Pilas</a> was attacked and driven from his city, and afterwards acknowledged the Kaan king as his overlord and ally against Tikal. In 657 Yuknoom defeated Tikal in a star war, after which Nuun Ujol Chaak seems to have pledged fealty. When Tikal ousted the Dos Pilas king in 672, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a> intervened and inflicted a second defeat on Nuun Ujol Chaak in 677, followed in 679 by a decisive defeat at the hands of Dos Pilas, almost certainly with Calakmul's aid. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naranjo">Naranjo</a>, after attacking Calakmul's client Caracol, had its royal lineage ended within two years.</p><p>Inscriptions at a number of sites recognised his overlordship. He sponsored three generations of Cancuen rulers and oversaw the accessions of two of them in 656 and 677. Far to the west, a king of Moral-Reforma was installed under Kaan auspices in 662, apparently in step with an attack by Piedras Negras on neighbouring Santa Elena, and an emissary of Yuknoom supervised a ritual at Piedras Negras in 685. A daughter of his married a lord of La Corona in 679.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His successor took the throne in 686, about fifty years after Yuknoom's accession, at the height of Kaan power over Tikal. Yuknoom was well into his eighties when he died, and many of the successes of his later years were probably the work of his successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuknoom-yichaak-kahk">Yuknoom Yich'aak K'ahk'</a>, who as a prince had already presided over a ritual attended by both rival lords of Mutal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yuknoom Chʼeen II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Worse came when Dos Pilas, an outpost ruled by a Tikal prince, B'alaj Chan K'awiil, went over to Kaanul: in 657 Calakmul's king <b>Yuknoom Ch'een II</b> drove Nuun Ujol Chaak from Tikal, and in 679 Dos Pilas defeated Tikal in battle.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tikal">Tikal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Yuknoom Ch'een II</b>, who reigned from 636 to about 686, it reached its height, and Calakmul holds more stelae than any other Maya city.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kaan">Kingdom of Kaan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.11,-89.81&amp;year=668" aria-label="Calakmul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Yuknoom Ch'een II"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5015 6010h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5015 6010h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.11,-89.81&amp;year=668">What was here in 668 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
